1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:02,473
Amy needed one new item code.

2
00:00:02,493 --> 00:00:09,558
It took 18 days, a bounced request and three approvers one at a time, and her sales order still failed.

3
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:14,496
At Lives Group, master data is created by email.

4
00:00:14,516 --> 00:00:19,666
Records arrive incomplete or duplicated, so the regional numbers can't be trusted.

5
00:00:19,686 --> 00:00:22,286
Tesseract fixes both ends.

6
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:30,346
Amy's item is a blood collection tube: a lab consumable regulated as an IVD, an in-vitro diagnostic device.

7
00:00:30,366 --> 00:00:33,695
She pastes her old email, and the form fills itself.

8
00:00:33,715 --> 00:00:40,492
As an IVD, it needs a licence number, and Quality joins the route automatically: the step she missed last time.

9
00:00:40,512 --> 00:00:48,997
Before submitting, she sees her approvers, all in parallel, and her go-live date: Tuesday 3 November.

10
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:53,240
Marcus is from a new business unit, Meridian Life Sciences.

11
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:57,815
He types a hospital in shorthand, and Tesseract finds it already on file.

12
00:00:57,835 --> 00:01:01,929
So he extends that record to his entity, instead of creating a duplicate.

13
00:01:01,949 --> 00:01:05,689
Business Support and Finance check it, then MDM.

14
00:01:05,950 --> 00:01:10,154
Wei Jie in Finance approves on a Teams card, where he already works.

15
00:01:10,174 --> 00:01:12,739
Credit terms never travel in the message.

16
00:01:12,759 --> 00:01:16,696
Only Finance and MDM can open them, inside Tesseract.

17
00:01:16,716 --> 00:01:21,512
Reminders go out before anything stalls, and every decision is logged.

18
00:01:21,870 --> 00:01:28,125
SAP Business One's built-in approvals are document-centric, designed around documents like sales orders.

19
00:01:28,145 --> 00:01:35,018
So approvals for master data live in Microsoft 365, and SAP stays the system of record.

20
00:01:35,038 --> 00:01:40,870
Priya clicks Generate SAP B1 import, and gets a Data Transfer Workbench file:

21
00:01:40,890 --> 00:01:45,734
the import format SAP already reads, with SAP's own field names.

22
00:01:45,754 --> 00:01:50,018
Sending it automatically, through SAP's Service Layer, is phase two.

23
00:01:50,038 --> 00:01:53,950
Today, she loads the file and marks the item created.

24
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:56,120
Amy is told the moment it's live.

25
00:01:56,140 --> 00:02:03,108
And stuck demand drops by S$36,000: three customer orders that were waiting on that one record.

26
00:02:03,870 --> 00:02:06,521
Now, money out, on synthetic data.

27
00:02:06,541 --> 00:02:13,596
Regional Pulse compares the 12 months to September 2026 with the year before, on de-duplicated customers.

28
00:02:13,616 --> 00:02:17,540
Orders are S$53.4 million, up 4%.

29
00:02:17,560 --> 00:02:24,057
Merge the duplicates, and Bukit Damai, Malaysia's #2 account, is really down 32%.

30
00:02:24,077 --> 00:02:28,208
Each country card names an owner, an action and the dollars at stake.

31
00:02:28,228 --> 00:02:36,106
On this illustrative data, today's forecasting method is off by 11 units per 100 ordered; ours, by 9.

32
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:41,261
Median setup drops from 14 days to ≤ 4 business days.

33
00:02:41,281 --> 00:02:46,443
About 1,750 hours a year come back, paying back in about 6 months.

34
00:02:46,463 --> 00:02:49,501
No new licences for the workflow in phase 1.

35
00:02:49,521 --> 00:02:50,509
One hospital.

36
00:02:50,529 --> 00:02:51,963
One record.
